If you don't care about the "id" column, which appears to be different across the various dataframes, you can do this with a join in dplyr, drop the IDs, and then filter out the NA's/missing values in the name column. Alternatively you can rename the id columns as "id_a", "id_b", "id_c" to keep better track of where data is coming from.

# using dplyr
library(dplyr)
joined_df <- left_join(a, b, by=c("name")) %>%
> left_join(., c, by=c("name")) %>%
> filter(name!="NA", name!="") %>%
> select(-starts_with("id"))

# OUTPUT
name   bd   si   mr
s 0.95 0.25 0.28
d 0.43 0.70 0.51
f 0.42 0.79 0.00
a 0.94 0.18 0.01
z 0.83 0.92   NA
x 0.17 0.17 0.09
7    v 0.93 0.99 0.88
8    b 0.24 0.85 0.12

Hope this helps,
Adios,
Ryan

